herdr guide 3: agents
=====================

After herdr-2-panes. Run several agents, find the ones that need you, and let one agent
drive others.

WATCH MANY AGENTS
  <prefix> g then b   List blocked agents (waiting at an approval or a question)
  <prefix> o          Jump to the latest notification
  A blocked agent does nothing until you answer in its pane. Answer it yourself. Do not let
    another agent approve for you.
  After a server restart, agents with a herdr integration resume by themselves. Check with:
    herdr integration status

START AND STEER AN AGENT FROM THE SHELL
  herdr pane split --current --direction right --no-focus   # read the pane id from the JSON
  herdr agent start reviewer --kind claude --pane <pane>   # kinds include kiro, claude, codex
  herdr agent prompt reviewer "<task>" --wait --timeout 600000
  herdr agent read reviewer --lines 80
  herdr agent get reviewer   # state: idle, working, blocked, done
  herdr agent send-keys reviewer esc
  herdr agent attach reviewer
  herdr agent list

ONE AGENT DRIVES OTHERS (LIAISON PATTERN)
  1. Start the liaison agent inside a herdr pane. The herdr skill needs HERDR_ENV=1.
  2. Name herdr in the prompt, or the skill does not load. Example: "Use herdr to start a
     claude reviewer in a pane to the right and give it this task: ..."
  3. Ask it to have each worker write its result to a file, and to name each worker.
  4. Ask it to report blocked workers to you, not to answer them.
  5. Watch the workers in their panes. Take over any pane at any time.

RULES FOR THE LIAISON BRIEF
  Use --no-focus, so your focus stays where it is
  Read pane and agent ids from the JSON output, never from sidebar order
  After a timeout or agent_prompt_stalled, read the agent before you resend
  Never close panes it did not create

REFRESH THE HERDR SKILL AFTER AN UPGRADE
  agents-sync         Rewrite the herdr skill for Claude Code, kiro and Codex from herdr
                      --skill
